{"id":"prop:zero-defect-reduction","kind":"proposition","statement":"Suppose a candidate \(k_0\) has \(\varepsilon_{H,k_0}=\varepsilon_{A,k_0}=\alpha_{k_0,n}=0\). If \(0<s_{k_0,n}\le1\) and \(\texttt{ass:grid-coverage}\) holds, then the deterministic grid functional satisfies
\[
 \inf_{\lambda\in\Lambda_n}
 \left\{\lambda^{\bar\beta}+\frac{s_{k_0,n}}{\lambda}\right\}
 \le3s_{k_0,n}^{\bar\beta/(1+\bar\beta)}.
\]
This algebraic optimization does not imply a rate for \(\widehat h\) under \(\mathcal M_n\): \(\texttt{thm:common-audit-oracle-impossibility}\) gives zero-defect configurations for which the displayed functional converges to zero while \(\|\widehat h-h^\dagger\|_{L_2(P_X)}^2=1/32\) almost surely. Independently, that theorem gives the deterministic clipping bound \(\|\widehat h-h^\dagger\|_{L_2(P_X)}^2\le4B^2\). The grid exponent equals \(\beta/(1+\beta)\) for \(\beta\le1\) and saturates at \(1/2\) for \(\beta>1\).","depends_on":["oeq:common-audit-oracle","ass:grid-coverage"],"status":"to-prove","justification":"The proposition preserves the valid dyadic bias--variance calculation without presenting it as an estimator rate.","gap":"The counterexample shows that a vanishing grid functional cannot compensate for a blind trained path.","consumer":"It records the saturated exponent for future positive results that add quantitative training-coverage control."}

